## What an Arduino library really is

An Arduino sketch is C++. When you press Upload, the IDE does a little preprocessing on the `.ino` file (it adds `#include <Arduino.h>` at the top and generates prototypes for your functions), saves it as a `.cpp`, and hands everything to `avr-g++` (on an Uno). Library files get **no** preprocessing: they are plain `.h` and `.cpp` files, so a library's `.cpp` must write `#include <Arduino.h>` itself if it uses `millis()` or `analogRead()`. That answers the first example's second question.

A library is just a folder the IDE knows to compile alongside your sketch. Put it in the `libraries` folder of your sketchbook (on your Mac that's `~/Documents/Arduino/libraries/`), restart the IDE, and `#include "Smooth.h"` works from any sketch. The modern layout (the "1.5 library format") looks like this:

```text
Smooth/
  library.properties      name, version, author, sentence, architectures ...
  keywords.txt            optional: words the IDE colours
  src/
    Smooth.h              the class declaration
    Smooth.cpp            the member function bodies
  examples/
    BasicSmoothing/
      BasicSmoothing.ino  appears under File > Examples > Smooth
```

`library.properties` is a short text file of `key=value` lines:

```text
name=Smooth
version=0.1.0
author=Mustafa
maintainer=Mustafa
sentence=Moving-average smoothing for noisy sensor readings.
paragraph=Fixed-size buffer, no heap allocation, works on an Uno.
category=Signal Input/Output
url=https://www.learncpp.com/
architectures=*
```

(Point `url` at your own repo once it exists.)

## The two design rules that matter more than the folder

**1. Keep the hardware out of the class.** A `MovingAverage` that calls `analogRead(A0)` inside itself can only be tested on a board, with a real sensor, by eye. A `MovingAverage` whose `add(int sample)` takes the number as an argument can be tested on your laptop with `assert`, in milliseconds. The sketch does the I/O; the class does the logic. This is the single habit that makes embedded C++ testable, and it's exactly how ROS 2 nodes are written later (the node does I/O, plain classes do the maths).

**2. No `new` on an Uno.** An ATmega328P has 2 KB of RAM. Heap allocation fragments that tiny space and fails silently. Use fixed-size arrays sized at compile time. Also remember `int` is **16 bits** on AVR: the sum of 64 readings of 1023 (65,472) overflows an `int`, so accumulate in `long` (32 bits).

## Worked example: a moving average

The trick is a *running sum*: when a new sample arrives, subtract the oldest one and add the new one, so `value()` is O(1) no matter the window size. A ring index (`next_`) wraps around the array.

```cpp
#include <cassert>
#include <iostream>

class MovingAverage {
public:
    void add(int sample) {
        sum_ -= buf_[next_];      // drop the oldest (0 while the buffer fills)
        buf_[next_] = sample;
        sum_ += sample;
        next_ = (next_ + 1) % kSize;
        if (count_ < kSize) ++count_;
    }
    int value() const { return count_ == 0 ? 0 : static_cast<int>(sum_ / count_); }
    int count() const { return count_; }
    void reset() {
        for (int& b : buf_) b = 0;
        sum_ = 0; next_ = 0; count_ = 0;
    }
private:
    static constexpr int kSize = 4;
    int buf_[kSize] = {};
    long sum_ = 0;
    int next_ = 0;
    int count_ = 0;
};

int main() {
    MovingAverage avg;
    assert(avg.value() == 0);
    avg.add(100);            // [100]           -> 100
    assert(avg.value() == 100);
    avg.add(200);            // [100,200]       -> 150
    assert(avg.value() == 150);
    avg.add(300); avg.add(400);   // full: 1000/4 -> 250
    assert(avg.value() == 250);
    avg.add(800);            // 100 drops out: (200+300+400+800)/4 = 425
    assert(avg.value() == 425);
    avg.reset();
    assert(avg.count() == 0);
    std::cout << "MovingAverage ok\n";
}
```

Trace the last step by hand once: the sum was 1000, `next_` points at slot 0 (holding 100), so the sum becomes 1000 − 100 + 800 = 1700, and 1700 / 4 = 425.

## Splitting it into the library

The header declares; the source defines. `#pragma once` (or a classic `#ifndef SMOOTH_H` guard) stops double inclusion within one translation unit.

```cpp-snippet
// src/Smooth.h
#pragma once

class MovingAverage {
public:
    void add(int sample);
    int value() const;
    int count() const;
    void reset();
private:
    static constexpr int kSize = 8;
    int buf_[kSize] = {};
    long sum_ = 0;
    int next_ = 0;
    int count_ = 0;
};
```

```cpp-snippet
// src/Smooth.cpp
#include "Smooth.h"

void MovingAverage::add(int sample) {
    sum_ -= buf_[next_];
    buf_[next_] = sample;
    sum_ += sample;
    next_ = (next_ + 1) % kSize;
    if (count_ < kSize) ++count_;
}
int MovingAverage::value() const { return count_ == 0 ? 0 : static_cast<int>(sum_ / count_); }
int MovingAverage::count() const { return count_; }
void MovingAverage::reset() { for (int& b : buf_) b = 0; sum_ = 0; next_ = 0; count_ = 0; }
```

And the example sketch, which does the I/O:

```arduino
#include <Smooth.h>

MovingAverage avg;

void setup() { Serial.begin(115200); }

void loop() {
  avg.add(analogRead(A0));
  Serial.print(analogRead(A0));
  Serial.print(' ');
  Serial.println(avg.value());   // two lines in the Serial Plotter
  delay(10);
}
```

Now the first example's first question: if the member function bodies sat in the header without `inline`, every `.cpp` that includes it would contain its own definition of `MovingAverage::add`. Each file compiles fine; the **linker** then sees two definitions and fails with "multiple definition". Functions defined *inside* the class body are implicitly `inline`, which is why the single-file version above is fine.

