steps.body:
## The problem templates solve

You've already been *using* templates since week 12: `std::vector<int>`, `std::array<double, 3>`, `std::unique_ptr<Node>`. Each is one piece of source code that the compiler stamps out for whatever type you put in the angle brackets. Today you write your own.

Without templates you'd write `maxInt`, `maxDouble`, `maxLong`: identical bodies, different types. Copy-paste code drifts apart the moment you fix a bug in one copy.

## Function templates

```cpp-snippet
template <typename T>
T biggest(T a, T b) { return a < b ? b : a; }
```

`T` is a placeholder for a type. When you call `biggest(3, 7)`, the compiler **deduces** `T = int` from the arguments and generates `int biggest(int, int)`. Call `biggest(2.5, 1.0)` and it generates a `double` version too. That generation is called **instantiation**, and it happens at compile time: there is no runtime cost and no type checking at runtime.

Deduction has one strict rule worth knowing early: every argument that uses `T` must agree. `biggest(3, 4.5)` asks for `T = int` from the first argument and `T = double` from the second, so deduction fails and the compiler refuses (that's the first example). You fix it by saying the type yourself: `biggest<double>(3, 4.5)` converts the `3` to `3.0`. That's not a quirk to memorise; it's the compiler declining to guess which conversion you meant.

The body must make sense for the type you use. `biggest` needs `operator<`. Call it with a type that has no `<` and you get an error **at the line inside the template**, reported "in instantiation of ...". Template errors are long; read the first line that mentions *your* file and *your* call, ignore the rest.

## Class templates and non-type parameters

A template parameter can also be a **value** known at compile time, like the `5` in `std::array<int, 5>`. That's exactly what Monday's smoothing class needs: element type *and* window size.

```cpp
#include <cassert>
#include <cstddef>
#include <iostream>

template <typename T, std::size_t N>
class RingAverage {
    static_assert(N > 0, "window must hold at least one sample");
public:
    void add(T sample) {
        sum_ -= buf_[next_];
        buf_[next_] = sample;
        sum_ += sample;
        next_ = (next_ + 1) % N;
        if (count_ < N) ++count_;
    }
    T value() const { return count_ == 0 ? T{} : sum_ / static_cast<T>(count_); }
    std::size_t size() const { return count_; }
    static constexpr std::size_t capacity() { return N; }
private:
    T buf_[N] = {};
    T sum_{};
    std::size_t next_ = 0;
    std::size_t count_ = 0;
};

template <typename T>
T clampTo(T v, T lo, T hi) { return v < lo ? lo : (hi < v ? hi : v); }

int main() {
    RingAverage<double, 3> d;
    d.add(1.0); d.add(2.0); d.add(6.0);
    assert(d.value() == 3.0);          // (1 + 2 + 6) / 3
    d.add(10.0);                        // 1.0 drops out: (2 + 6 + 10) / 3 = 6
    assert(d.value() == 6.0);

    RingAverage<long, 4> l;
    for (long x : {100L, 200L, 300L, 401L}) l.add(x);
    assert(l.value() == 250);           // 1001 / 4 = 250 in integer division
    static_assert(RingAverage<int, 16>::capacity() == 16);

    assert(clampTo(15, 0, 10) == 10);
    assert(clampTo<double>(-0.5, 0, 1) == 0.0);  // explicit T: 0 and 1 become doubles
    std::cout << "templates ok\n";
}
```

Notice three things. `T{}` means "a value-initialised T" (0 for numbers), so the class works for `int`, `long`, `float` or `double`. `static_assert` turns a nonsense instantiation (`RingAverage<int, 0>`) into a clear compile-time message. And `RingAverage<double, 3>` and `RingAverage<long, 4>` are **two completely different classes** to the compiler; each gets its own generated code. On an Uno that matters: every distinct instantiation costs flash memory, so don't instantiate ten variants for fun.

## Why templates live in headers

On Monday you put declarations in `Smooth.h` and bodies in `Smooth.cpp`. That doesn't work for templates. When `main.cpp` writes `RingAverage<float, 16>`, the compiler must generate the code *right there*, which means it must see the full body. If the body sits in `Ring.cpp`, the compiler of `main.cpp` can't see it, and the compiler of `Ring.cpp` doesn't know anyone wants a `float, 16` version. Result: "undefined reference" at link time. So template libraries are usually **header-only**: everything in the `.h`. Many Arduino libraries you'll download are header-only for exactly this reason.

## On the Arduino

Templates work on AVR because they're a compiler feature, not a library feature. What the classic AVR toolchain lacks is most of the **standard library** (`<vector>`, `<algorithm>`, `std::min`). That's one reason small embedded code bases write their own tiny templated containers, like `RingAverage`, instead of reaching for `std::vector`.

