**Part 1: simulator evidence (30 min).** Do the task below. Run a small grid (kp in 5, 10, 20; kd in 0, 0.3, 1, 2) and print settling time and worst overshoot for each. Paste the table into `logs/tuning.md`.

**Part 2: PD on the robot (35 min).** Replace yesterday's one-liner with a PD update (tomorrow's C++ lesson turns this into a tested `Pid` class you can drop in on Saturday). Error in sensor units (−2000..2000), output in PWM:

```arduino
const float KP = 0.08, KD = 0.002;   // starting values
int lastError = 0;
unsigned long lastTime = 0;
// every 10 ms:
float dt = (now - lastTime) / 1000.0;
lastTime = now;
float corr = KP * error + KD * (error - lastError) / dt;
lastError = error;
corr = constrain(corr, -BASE, BASE);
setMotors(BASE + corr, BASE - corr);
```

Note the size of KD: with dt = 0.01 s, a change of 200 position units per step is a derivative of 20,000/s, so KD = 0.002 already contributes 40 PWM. Raise BASE from 80 to 110 and retune: KP up until it wiggles, then KD up until the wiggle dies. If the motors buzz or twitch, D is amplifying sensor noise; smooth the position (Monday's `RingAverage` with 2–3 samples) or reduce KD.

Build a **tuning rig** in Wokwi: a virtual line follower inside the Uno, with **two potentiometers** as live gain knobs (A0 → Kp from 0 to 0.2, A1 → Kd from 0 to 0.01). The sketch keeps a virtual robot state (offset `y` in sensor units, heading), computes the error, applies your PD output to two virtual wheels with a lag, and prints `error:` for the plotter. Use the same model as your Python simulator from yesterday's build session (lookahead, motor lag), scaled to sensor units.

```arduino
float y = 1500, heading = 0, vl = 0, vr = 0, lastErr = 0;
const float BASE = 80, DT = 0.01, TAU = 0.08, LOOK = 1.0;
unsigned long last = 0;
void setup() { Serial.begin(115200); }
void loop() {
  if (millis() - last < 10) return;
  last = millis();
  float kp = analogRead(A0) * 0.2 / 1023, kd = analogRead(A1) * 0.01 / 1023;
  float err = constrain(y + LOOK * heading, -2000, 2000);
  float corr = kp * err + kd * (err - lastErr) / DT;
  lastErr = err;
  vl += (BASE + corr - vl) * DT / TAU;
  vr += (BASE - corr - vr) * DT / TAU;
  heading += (vr - vl) * DT * 2.0;        // turn rate scale for the virtual robot
  y += heading * DT * 10.0;               // sideways drift from heading
  Serial.print("error:"); Serial.println(err);
  if (Serial.available()) { Serial.read(); y = 1500; }   // send any key to kick it off the line again
}
```

The scale factors are made up to give a robot-like response; what matters is the procedure. Follow steps 3 and 4 with the knobs: Kd at zero, raise Kp until steady oscillation, then raise Kd until it settles in one or two swings. Kick it (send a key) after each change. Record the knob values.

## Why a build system, and why CMake

Typing `g++ a.cpp b.cpp c.cpp -Iinclude -o app` works until it doesn't: you recompile everything after each change, you forget a flag, and the command doesn't run on another OS. A build system tracks what depends on what and only rebuilds what changed. **CMake** sits one level higher: you describe your project once in `CMakeLists.txt`, and CMake *generates* the real build files (Makefiles or Ninja files on macOS and Linux, Xcode or Visual Studio projects if you ask for them).

The reason it's in this course: **ROS 2 C++ packages are built with CMake** (through `ament_cmake`). Every `rclcpp` node you write from spring onwards has a `CMakeLists.txt`. Learn the core now, on a project you understand, and those files will look familiar.

## The mental model: targets

Modern CMake is about **targets**: a library or an executable, plus the properties attached to it (its sources, include directories, compile options, what it links to). You don't set global flags; you attach things to targets.

```text
control/
  CMakeLists.txt
  include/control/pid.h
  src/pid.cpp
  tests/test_pid.cpp
```

```cmake
cmake_minimum_required(VERSION 3.20)
project(control LANGUAGES CXX)

set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD 20)
set(CMAKE_CXX_STANDARD_REQUIRED ON)

add_library(control src/pid.cpp)
target_include_directories(control PUBLIC include)

add_executable(test_pid tests/test_pid.cpp)
target_link_libraries(test_pid PRIVATE control)

enable_testing()
add_test(NAME pid COMMAND test_pid)
```

Line by line: `add_library` makes a target from sources. `target_include_directories(control PUBLIC include)` says "to compile `control`, look in `include/`, **and** anyone who links `control` should look there too." That's what **PUBLIC** means: the requirement propagates to users. **PRIVATE** means only this target needs it (the test program needs `control` to build, but nothing links against the test). **INTERFACE** means only users need it (common for header-only libraries like Wednesday's `RingAverage`: `add_library(smooth INTERFACE)`). Because `test_pid` links `control`, it inherits the include path automatically; you never write `-Iinclude` yourself.

## Running it

On the Mac, install CMake once with Homebrew: `brew install cmake` (check with `cmake --version`). The compiler it finds is Apple clang, which also answers to `g++`/`c++`; everything below is standard C++20 and builds the same on the Ubuntu machine you'll use for ROS 2.

Always build **out of source**: the generated mess goes in `build/`, never next to your code, and `build/` goes in `.gitignore`.

```bash
cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug   # configure: generate build files
cmake --build build                            # compile only what changed
ctest --test-dir build --output-on-failure     # run every add_test
```

Change one line of `pid.cpp` and run the build again: only `pid.cpp` recompiles and things are relinked. `Debug` adds `-g` for the debugger (week 8); `Release` turns on optimisation.

## The code we're building: a PID class

You meet PID control in robotics on Monday evening; here's the code side. A PID controller turns an error (setpoint − measurement) into a command:

output = Kp·e + Ki·∑e·dt + Kd·(e − e_prev)/dt

clamped to what the actuator can do (a PWM value of −255..255). Following Monday's rule, it takes the error as an argument and never touches hardware, so it compiles for the laptop and for the Uno.

```cpp
#include <cassert>
#include <cmath>
#include <iostream>

class Pid {
public:
    Pid(double kp, double ki, double kd) : kp_(kp), ki_(ki), kd_(kd) {}
    void setLimits(double lo, double hi) { lo_ = lo; hi_ = hi; }
    double update(double error, double dt) {
        integral_ += error * dt;
        double deriv = first_ ? 0.0 : (error - prev_) / dt;
        first_ = false;
        prev_ = error;
        double out = kp_ * error + ki_ * integral_ + kd_ * deriv;
        if (out > hi_) out = hi_;
        if (out < lo_) out = lo_;
        return out;
    }
    void reset() { integral_ = 0; prev_ = 0; first_ = true; }
private:
    double kp_, ki_, kd_;
    double lo_ = -1e9, hi_ = 1e9;
    double integral_ = 0, prev_ = 0;
    bool first_ = true;
};

int main() {
    Pid p(2.0, 0.5, 0.1);
    // first call: P = 2*1 = 2, I = 0.5*(1*0.1) = 0.05, D = 0 (no previous error)
    assert(std::fabs(p.update(1.0, 0.1) - 2.05) < 1e-9);
    // second call, error 0.5: P = 1, I = 0.5*0.15 = 0.075, D = 0.1*(0.5-1)/0.1 = -0.5
    assert(std::fabs(p.update(0.5, 0.1) - 0.575) < 1e-9);
    p.setLimits(-1, 1);
    assert(p.update(10.0, 0.1) == 1.0);    // clamped
    std::cout << "pid ok\n";
}
```

Check the second call by hand: the integral is 0.1 + 0.05 = 0.15, so I = 0.5 × 0.15 = 0.075; P = 2 × 0.5 = 1; D = 0.1 × (0.5 − 1) / 0.1 = −0.5. Total 0.575. Skipping the derivative on the first call avoids a huge spike from a made-up previous error of 0 (the "derivative kick").

In the real layout, `Pid`'s declaration goes in `include/control/pid.h`, the bodies in `src/pid.cpp`, and those asserts become `tests/test_pid.cpp`. The same `pid.h`/`pid.cpp` pair can be copied into an Arduino library's `src/` unchanged; that's the payoff of keeping hardware out.

